  topic: Public Comment on Agenda Items
  desc: Citizens address the board on agenda items, with the vast majority of comments
    focused on the SPHS Athletic Complex proposal. Speakers present arguments both
    for and against the use of artificial turf, and one principal offers a tribute
    to a retiring colleague.
  children:
  - seconds: 2983
    speaker: Aaron Martin
    text: A state-licensed geologist and environmental scientist, speaks in favor
      of the proposed turf, clarifying that the new materials do not contain the contaminants
      of older systems. He states he has no health concerns and argues an engineered
      system is necessary for a heavily used athletic complex.
  - seconds: 3191
    speaker: Jen Swift
    text: A parent of a football player, speaks in favor of turf, sharing that her
      son developed a knee infection from goose feces on the current grass field.
      She believes a turf field would provide a healthier and safer environment for
      student athletes.
  - seconds: 3325
    speaker: Amy Huskins
    text: A citizen expresses concern about the plastic waste generated by turf fields
      and questions the viability of recycling options, noting only 50 of 13,000 fields
      have been recycled. She urges the board to see a complete proposal for a natural
      grass option and hear from experts on that side of the issue.
  - seconds: 3495
    speaker: Beth Myers
    text: A citizen echoes the call to fully explore natural grass options, suggesting
      the board speak with other schools that successfully maintain grass fields.
      She questions if new grass cultivars and a higher tolerance for certain weeds
      have been considered to make a natural field more viable.
  - seconds: 3663
    speaker: Joe Lawler
    text: A citizen raises the concern of voter approval, noting the previous bond
      was rejected substantially. He suggests the board consider putting two proposals
      on the ballot—one with turf and one without—to see what voters will actually
      approve and avoid further delays.
  - seconds: 3773
    speaker: Susan Tompkins
    text: A parent of a track athlete expresses sadness that the school can no longer
      host home track meets due to the facility's condition. She urges the board to
      move forward with a proposal that can pass in June to avoid further deterioration
      and loss of opportunities for students.
  - seconds: 3921
    speaker: Karen Taino
    text: A citizen questions the definition of "recycling" being used for turf fields,
      arguing that the process described is incineration, which Maine law defines
      as solid waste disposal, not recycling. She asks the board to press for clarity
      on what percentage of the material is truly recycled versus burned.
  - seconds: 4120
    speaker: Ralph Myers
    text: A citizen raises questions about the proposed Brockville infill, asking
      what treatments will be necessary to prevent mold and bacteria growth in the
      wood-based material over time. He also expresses concern about water runoff
      from the artificial field during heavy rain.
  - seconds: 4312
    speaker: Bethany Connolly
    text: 'The principal of Skillin Elementary speaks on behalf of the district''s
      administrative team to wish retiring principal Diane Lang happiness in her next
      chapter. She describes Ms. Lang as an amazing colleague, friend, and leader
      who keeps everyone focused on what is most important: the kids.'
  - seconds: 4425
    speaker: Jen Barca
    text: A citizen argues that the grass vs. turf issue is not black and white, citing
      the case of Bonny Eagle High School, where grass athletic fields contaminated
      the school's drinking water with PFAS. She urges the board to find a realistic,
      optimal solution that provides usable fields for the most students now, rather
      than delaying for a perfect but unattainable option.
